; **RAMPS, where this arena had two ten-step staircases.** Asked for to make the
; traversal easier, and they suit the modal controller far better than steps did:
; a charge up a ramp is a real decision, where a charge into a step is a stumble.
;
; 21.8 degrees — an 8 m rise over a 20 m run — and the angle is the whole point.
; Steeper than about 27 and you slide back down standing still, because the slope
; pull beats `deceleration`. Shallower than about 7 and momentum stops building.
; 21.8 sits between the two, so the ramp is climbable AND worth charging down.
;
; The top surface satisfies y = 4 + 0.4 * (z + 30), so it passes through y = 8 at
; z = -20 (the deck's south edge) and y = 0 at z = -40. `tests/warden_probe.gd`
; checks it against that line rather than by eye, because a hand-written rotation
; has come out wrong every single time this project has authored one — and this
; one was no exception. The first attempt used origin y = 3.3036, derived from
; `cos(21.8) * half-thickness`, and measured 0.11 m high at every point.
;
; The missing term: rotating the box moves its top face in Z as well as Y, so
; the point of the surface that is directly above a given global z is not the
; local centre of the face. The offset is `sin(21.8) * half-thickness` along z,
; which at this gradient is another 0.11 m of height. Invisible by eye, caught
; by the probe in one run.
